fopen honours '+', so a stream can be written and then read.

MEASURED. tcc's `ar` writes its members to a scratch file and copies them
back:

    fo = fopen(tfile, "wb+");                       tcctools.c:184
    ...
    fseek(fo, 0, SEEK_END); fsize = ftell(fo);
    fseek(fo, 0, SEEK_SET); fread(buf, fsize, 1, fo);
    fwrite(buf, fsize, 1, fh);                      tcctools.c:317

fopen looked only at mode[0], so "wb+" opened O_WRONLY. fgetc refuses a
write-mode stream outright --

    if(O_WRONLY == f->bufmode) return EOF;

-- so fread returned 0 and never touched `buf`. NOTHING REPORTED AN ERROR
ANYWHERE. tcc then wrote out whatever `buf` happened to contain, and because
the previous allocation had just been freed and both sizes round into the same
power-of-two class, that was the OBJECT FILE MINUS ITS SIXTY-BYTE MEMBER
HEADER. The archive came out the right length, structurally wrong, and mc-tcc
rejected its own output:

    mc-tcc -ar libh.a h1.o   ->  1182 bytes, the control's size exactly
    mc-tcc ... libh.a        ->  libh.a: error: invalid archive

A silent read failure that produces plausible bytes is the worst shape a
runtime gap can have, which is why this is written down at length.

WHAT '+' NOW MEANS HERE. A third bufmode, O_RDWR: output still goes through
the buffer exactly as before, and reads go straight to the descriptor after a
flush. Reading out of the buffer would hand back the bytes waiting to be
written, and flushing first also makes the descriptor's position mean what
ftell and fseek report.

  fgetc   flush, then read(2) one byte from the descriptor
  ftell   flush, then lseek(fd, 0, SEEK_CUR) -- file_pos counts only what was
          written, and this stream is also being read
  fseek   flush, then lseek, the same route O_WRONLY already took

ONLY 'w+' AND 'a+'. A read stream here slurps the whole file into a buffer at
open time, so "r+" would have to write back through that buffer as well -- a
different design, and nothing on this path needs it. It is unchanged.

MEASURED AFTER, with micro-c's sizeof fix beside it (EXPERIMENT-zzzr):

    system `ar t` lists the members, `nm` finds the symbols
    mc-tcc links against its own single-object archive   -> 5, correct
    mc-tcc links against its own three-object archive    -> 6, correct

    difftest amd64        98 pass, 0 fail, 2 known gaps      unchanged
    difftest aarch64     100 pass, 0 fail, 2 known gaps      unchanged
    stage 2 corpus       419 of 426                          unchanged
    tests2 through mc-tcc 90 of 105                          unchanged
    self-compilation      gen2.o == gen3.o == gen4.o         unchanged

--- a/stdio.c
+++ b/stdio.c
@@ -83,6 +83,18 @@ int fgetc(FILE* f)
 	/* Only read on read buffers */
 	if(O_WRONLY == f->bufmode) return EOF;
 
+	/* A '+' STREAM IS WRITTEN THROUGH THE BUFFER AND READ THROUGH THE
+	 * DESCRIPTOR. Its buffer holds pending output, so reading out of it
+	 * would hand back the bytes waiting to be written. Flushing first also
+	 * makes the descriptor's position mean what the caller thinks it does. */
+	if(O_RDWR == f->bufmode)
+	{
+		char one;
+		fflush(f);
+		if(1 != read(f->fd, &one, 1)) return EOF;
+		return one & 0xFF;
+	}
